/*!
    \page index.html
    \title VSMM Developer Reference
    \brief Reference for the C++ backend of the Vintage Story Mod Manager and the API it exposes to QML.

    The backend is split into small libraries. Each library that QML uses is
    also its own QML module and registers one singleton.

    \section1 Using the backend from QML

    The app module \c vsmm lists every backend module in its \c IMPORTS, so a
    QML file in the app needs only one import. Every singleton below is then
    reachable by name:

    \qml
    import vsmm

    Label { text: ModStore.installedModsCount }
    \endqml

    The engine creates the singletons when the main QML file loads.
    \l vsmm::App then connects them to each other and calls \c validate() on
    Config, before the event loop starts.

    \table
    \header \li Singleton \li QML module \li Use it for
    \row \li \l [QML] {Config} \li Config \li Reading and writing user settings.
    \row \li \l [QML] {ModStore} \li ModStore \li Mod counts, selection, updates and removal.
    \row \li \l [QML] {ModSortFilterModel} \li ModSortFilterModel \li Sorted and filtered model of the installed mods.
    \row \li \l [QML] {ModListModel} \li ModListModel \li Source model, defines the roles of each mod row.
    \row \li \l [QML] {ModUpdateModel} \li ModUpdateModel \li Progress of the current update batch.
    \row \li \l [QML] {GameMngr} \li GameMngr \li Launching the game, game version, disabled mods.
    \row \li \l [QML] {ModLoader} \li ModLoader \li Reading and installing a mod zip from a file.
    \endtable

    \section1 Mod icons

    The \c modicon image provider is registered on the engine before the
    main QML file loads. Its URLs have this form:

    \badcode
    image://modicon/<modId>?url=<percent-encoded logo url>
    \endcode

    Bind to the \c modThumbnail role of \l [QML] {ModListModel}
    instead of building the URL. The model encodes the nested URL and returns
    an empty string while no icon is known:

    \qml
    Image {
        source: modThumbnail
        visible: modThumbnail !== ""
    }
    \endqml

    \section1 Context properties

    \c App sets three \c bool context properties on the root context:
    \c IS_LINUX, \c IS_WINDOWS and \c IS_MACOS. They are compile time
    constants for the platform VSMM was built for. Context properties are
    untyped and unknown to qmlls, so using one gives an unqualified access
    warning.

    \section1 All types

    \list
    \li \l {VSMM QML Types}
    \li \l {VSMM C++ Classes}
    \endlist
*/

